# Prospective launch review: CPU profile v3

**Verdict: APPROVE exactly one bounded native-emitter CPU-profile diagnostic launch under these bindings.** This is launch approval only; it grants no native acceptance, build/runtime result, performance, parity, memory, or adoption credit.

The prior v2 raw stderr contains the cooperative-stop marker, but its profile is absent at the 30-second emitter cap; the receipt classifies the run incomplete and reports restoration completed. The changed experiment is narrow: the candidate cooperative cutoff moves from 25 to 20 seconds, and the pinned Bun 1.3.14 command adds --cpu-prof-interval=10000. The captured Bun help identifies this option as a sampling interval in microseconds with a 1000-microsecond default. This lowers sample frequency and gives five more seconds before the unchanged cap. This is a plausible bounded retry after new evidence; it does not establish why the prior profile was missing or guarantee that this one will flush.

I inspected the v2/v3 candidate, native-plan, and outer-sequence diffs; verified the option against the captured pinned Bun help; checked the source scope, freshness and restoration guards, and recomputed the bindings. The candidate diff changes only the cutoff comparison. The existing outer sequence is unchanged. All 171 outer-plan pins match; the 53-file source inventory, full 207,834-byte oracle, CPU 3, preactivation absent-and-not-symlink profile check, and restore/verify-before-unlock sequence remain bound. Source/JS evidence remains exact scoped reuse only. Stage caps remain 30/120/5 seconds. A missing or invalid profile stays a defined diagnostic failure; inspect and hash/parse any output after terminal restoration before consumption.

